{
  "0": "Embedding representation: apply smooth row-norm compression e'=e/sqrt(1+||e||²/d) to existing item embeddings, preserving dimensions and tying. This may reduce domination by large-norm items while retaining directional information, especially in Electronics. Keep features, GRU topology, readout, scoring formula, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply across all four datasets with identical normalization in training and the predictor writer; retain one model per dataset.",
  "1": "Network architecture: insert one shared positionwise residual bottleneck after the GRU states: h'=h+W2 GELU(W1 h), with bottleneck width max(8, hidden_size/4) and zero-initialized W2. This inexpensive internal layer may refine recurrent representations while initially preserving baseline behavior. Keep embeddings, history, existing readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed across all four datasets. Update the predictor writer to load this single jointly trained network per dataset.",
  "2": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.8*h_last+0.2*m, where m is the padding-masked mean of existing GRU states weighted by 2^(-age/10), and age counts valid interactions backward from the last state. This modest history contribution may stabilize recent intent without overwhelming it. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four datasets. Match training and predictor-writer readouts within one model per dataset.",
  "3": "Training strategy: keep the current optimizer and initial learning rate, but decay each parameter group's learning rate after every epoch by 0.97, with a floor of 0.25 times its initial rate. Smoother late updates may reduce the observed validation oscillation while retaining early learning speed. Preserve architecture, features, loss, batch size, dropout, checkpoint selection and stopping rules across all four datasets; keep seed 42 and epoch cap 200, loading one checkpoint per dataset."
}
